gcshape 1  into:/Game/GraphCodeMarket/GraphCode/Rope  smooth:0.5  shade:40

# ROPE (TK15, 21 Nov 2026; rounds in `Docs/GC-SHAPE-DAMAGE-KIT-ROUNDS.md` "TK15", photos `Saved/Gate/Reference/Rope/`): a
# laid rope and what rope does: a straight length, a span sagging between two points, a hanging end that lies on the
# ground, a flat coil, turns round a member, a SQUARE lashing of two crossing members, a ROUND lashing of two side by
# side, a hitch tied off on a post, an overhand knot. Each is one rope swept along a path worked out by formula (no
# physics, no draping: nothing here rests on what is under it except by its own numbers). Any OTHER rope, resting on
# what it meets, is a path you declare and drape: ANY ROPE, ITS PATH DECLARED, below.
#
#   Rp   = shape('/GraphCodeShape/Presets/Rope', Rope = $Hemp)
#   Tie  = mesh() { add($Rp.Square, r=0.5, aw=6.0, ah=6.0, bw=10.0, bh=10.0, turns=4, frap=2, age=0.8) }
#   Line = mesh() { add($Rp.Span, r=0.8, span=250.0, sag=40.0, age=1.2) }
#
# THE ROPE: every piece takes these, then its own (below). Lengths in cm. The slot `Rope` is pathless: bind it.
#   r          0.6 cm     [0.1..5]    rope radius (a 12 mm rope is 0.6)
#   strands      3 count  [0, 2..6]   strands laid round the axis; 0 one plain round cord
#   lay        3.0 x d    [2..8]      lay length (one strand's full turn) in rope diameters; the photos read 3.3 at
#                                     the crowns; 3.0 gives the strands their 30-45 degree look
#   hand         1 +1/-1              +1 right-hand (Z) lay, -1 left-hand (S)
#   budget       1 0-2                0 game: a plain tube of 6 sides, coarse on bends; 1 high: laid strands of 8 sides
#                                     (a plain rope, strands=0, of 10); 2 ultra: strands of 10 sides, finer, loose fibres
#   sides       -1 count  [3..16]     the round's sides (of each strand, or of a plain rope); -1 from the budget
#   detail       1 x      [0.6..4]    stations along the rope, x the budget's (below 0.6 a bend turns to edges)
#   age          0 0-2                0 new; 1 used: ends frayed 1.2 d, a few fibres up; 2 old: ends frayed 4.8 d, strands
#                                     uneven, one strand broken, lashings, wraps and spans slacker
#   fray        -1 x d    [0..10]     frayed length at a free end; -1 from age (1.2 x age squared)
#   fuzz        -1 per 10 cm [0..20]  loose fibres (budget 2); -1 from age (0.6 + 1.2 x age)
#   lost        -1 0/1                one strand broken part way; -1 from age (past 1.5)
#   noise        1                    the seed; `index` this piece's number (two pieces of one seed differ by index)
#
# THE PIECES (their own knobs; every frame is the piece's own, place it with at=)
#   Line(len=100, sp=0)                along +Y from the origin. `sp` > 0: the stations along it exactly `sp` cm apart
#                                      (rounded to fit the length), for a Line `follow()` bends round a lashing; 0 (the
#                                      default): as before, 8 along a plain rope, the lay's own along a laid one
#   Span(span=200, rise=0, sag=20, length=0, slack=1)   a catenary from (0,0,0) to (0,span,rise): `sag` below the
#                                      chord at mid span, or `length` (rope length, wins when > 0); `slack` x age adds sag
#   Hang(len=200, drop=150, bend=-1, sway=0, wander=0)  down from the origin to the ground `drop` below, bending
#                                      (radius `bend`, -1 = 8r or 0.08 drop) onto it along +Y; `sway` cm sideways on the
#                                      drop, `wander` cm side to side on the ground
#   Coil(turns=5, inner=0.8, gap=1, tail=40)  a flat (Flemish) coil on the ground round the origin, from the inside out:
#                                      `inner` the centre hole in rope diameters, `gap` turn pitch in rope widths, `tail`
#                                      the end leaving straight
#   Wrap(w=6, h=6, corner=-1, turns=4, gap=1, slack=1, axis=0, tuck=1)  turns round a member of section w x h (corner,
#                                      -1 = round) along Y, from y = 0, stepping `gap` rope widths a turn; `axis` 1 lays the
#                                      member along X, 2 stands it along Z, 3 along -X; `tuck` 1 sinks both ends under
#   Turn(w=6, h=6, corner=-1, slack=1, axis=0)  one closed ring round a member or a bundle
#   Square(aw=8, ah=8, ac=-1, bw=8, bh=8, bc=-1, turns=4, frap=2, hitch=1, tail=6, slack=1, band=-1, aside=1, bside=-1,
#          sink=0, press=-1)           a SQUARE LASHING: member A along X (aw across, ah tall, axis at the origin) on top of
#                                      member B along Y (bw x bh) behind it, the two touching (`sink` cm when A sits into
#                                      B). `turns` over A each side of B and behind B each side of A, each a rope width
#                                      out; `frap` frapping turns between the members round the turns; `hitch` 1 a hitch
#                                      on B (on the `bside` side, -1 = -Y) and one on A (`aside`, +1 = +X), their ends
#                                      `tail` long; `band` 1 lays the turns as one flat band (it twists on the spans; 0 default); `press` >= 0
#                                      flattens the rope where it passes over A's top `press` cm above it (a deck laid on;
#                                      it stretches the texture there). Turns stand off a square arris to a bend of 3r
#   Round(aw=8, bw=8, gap=-1, turns=6, frap=2, slack=1, band=-1)  a ROUND (shear) LASHING of two members along Y side by
#                                      side, `gap` apart (-1 = 2.2r): turns round both from y = 0, frapping between them
#   Hitch(w=6, h=6, corner=-1, stand=10, tail=6, axis=0)  two turns and a riding end round a member along Y; `stand` the
#                                      standing part (along +X), `tail` the end
#   PostHitch(post=12, stand=60, tail=8)  the hitch round an upright post of diameter `post`, its standing part level
#   Overhand(tail=8, tail2=8)          an overhand knot, its two tails
#
# ANY ROPE, ITS PATH DECLARED (21 Nov 2026, `Docs/GC-SHAPE-DRAPE.md` "PATH WORDS"): the pieces above are worked out by
# formula; any other rope is a PATH you write in a `plane()`, word by word or point by point, that `drape` pulls onto
# what it rests on and a `Line` then follows. No tie is a preset: a tie is its path. The words append guide points in
# the order written and mix with raw points: around, over, under, at, between, tuck, to, and a 3D points() after a word
# (ask each: `gc_describe kind:"shape" block:"around"`). Every point on a member is read from its REAL section (crooked,
# tapered, oval, square) and stands `off` rope radii out; `t=` (0..1) or `at=$Other` (+ `beside=`, `ring=`) says where;
# `over` is the side seen from `up=` (default +Z), `under` the side away; `hold` holds a word's points. Short examples:
#
#   SQUARE (A over B, crossing)  Lash = plane(r=0.5) {
#                                  at($B, at=$A, beside=-1, ring=7, angle=60, r=$r, off=0.5, hold)
#                                  at($B, at=$A, beside=-1, ring=1.5, angle=60, off=0.5, hold)
#                                  loop (repeat=4) {
#                                    over($A, at=$B, beside=1, ring=output.index)  under($B, at=$A, beside=1, ring=output.index)
#                                    over($A, at=$B, beside=-1, ring=output.index)  under($B, at=$A, beside=-1, ring=output.index)
#                                  }
#                                  between($A, $B, turns=2)  tuck(len=4)
#                                  drape(onto=($A, $B), r=$r, taut, self)
#                                }
#                                Tie = mesh() { add($Rp.Line, r=0.5, len=520, sp=1.2, join=none) follow(along=$Lash) }
#   WRAP (turns round one pole)  around($Pole, t=0.5, turns=8, side=-1, r=0.4) after a start laid along it
#                                (`at($Pole, t=0.62, angle=150, off=0.5, hold)` twice), then tuck(len=5), drape(onto=$Pole, taut, self)
#   ROUND (two poles touching)   around($P1, $P2, t=0.43, turns=7) between two `at(..., hold)`s, tuck(len=6)
#   SHEAR (two poles apart)      around($P1, $P2, turns=6)  between($P1, $P2, turns=2)  tuck(len=5)
#   BIND (a bundle of sticks)    around($S1, $S2, $S3, turns=5)  tuck(len=4)
#   OVER A BEAM (thrown over)    to((-30, 0, 60), r=0.8)  over($Beam)  to((30, 0, 40))
#                                drape(onto=$Beam, r=0.8, length=260, pin=(), friction=0.8)
#   HANG (raw points only)       points((0, 0, 200), (300, 0, 180))  drape(r=0.6, slack=1.12)
#   THE LOWEST LEVEL             a loop writes `$g[i] = (x, y, z)`, then `points($g)`; words may follow it
#
#
# BUILT ON: `Core` (the rope along +Y, s = 0..len), `Strand`, `Seg`, `Whisker`; formulas Q* (a rounded rectangle's
# half loop by length), F* (the full loop), W* (wrap), S*/V*/L* (square lashing), FW*/G* (frapping), K* (hitch), O*
# (overhand), Cat* (catenary), Co* (coil), H* (a strand's helix, its frame turned so it does not twist), Fw (fray).
# A formula is written out where it is read: lengths that do not change along the rope are worked out once in the
# entry and passed in (a first square lashing that recomputed them read 3.4 million characters a point and stalled
# the editor).

build {
  SM_Rope_Line_Game       = $SampleLineGame     [pivot=(0, 0, 0), triangles=400]
  SM_Rope_Line_High       = $SampleLineHigh     [pivot=(0, 0, 0)]
  SM_Rope_Span_High       = $SampleSpanHigh     [pivot=(0, 0, 0)]
  SM_Rope_Coil_High       = $SampleCoilHigh     [pivot=(0, 0, 0)]
  SM_Rope_Square_Game     = $SampleSquareGame   [pivot=(0, 0, 0), triangles=2000]
  SM_Rope_Square_High     = $SampleSquareHigh   [pivot=(0, 0, 0)]
  SM_Rope_Overhand_High   = $SampleKnotHigh     [pivot=(0, 0, 0)]
}

Rope = material()

Hash = formula(s, c) { sin($s * 12.9898 + $c) * 43758.5453 - floor(sin($s * 12.9898 + $c) * 43758.5453) }
Ln = formula(v) { 1000000 * (pow(max($v, 0.000000001), 0.000001) - 1) }
Asinh = formula(u) { clamp($u * 1000000000, -1, 1) * $Ln(abs($u) + sqrt($u * $u + 1)) }
Sinh = formula(u) { 0.5 * (pow(2.718281828459045, $u) - pow(2.718281828459045, -$u)) }
Cosh = formula(u) { 0.5 * (pow(2.718281828459045, $u) + pow(2.718281828459045, -$u)) }

